{
  "case_id": "2016-16-405",
  "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0000-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We'll hear argument next this morning in Case 16-405, BNSF Railway v. Tyrrell."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "BNSF Railway (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Montana Supreme Court concluded that BNSF is subject to general personal jurisdiction in Montana for two reasons: First, it held that Montana State courts exercise general all-purpose jurisdiction over BNSF because BNSF does systematic and continuous business activity in the State. Second, it held that Section 56 of the Federal Employers' Liability Act confers personal jurisdiction on State courts. Both conclusions were wrong."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: I'm sorry. Didn't it register in Montana?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondents says that adopting your position would create chaos in the industry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So there are two questions before the Court. The Court said in Omni that in order to assert personal jurisdiction, you have to have a statute or a rule making the defendant amenable to service of process."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: \"Concurrent\" usually means that it's not -- it's a Federal claim, but it could be adjudicated in State as well as Federal court. That's what concurrent jurisdiction means."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "BNSF",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MURRAY: Well, I -- we think, Your Honor, in this context it is ambiguous, given -- if you look at the statute as a package where it has the \"may\" -- \"may be brought\" language, the \"concurrent jurisdiction\" language, and then the very unusual provision that actually prohibits removal of these State -- of these Federal causes of action into Federal court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "BNSF",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would point, Your Honor -- I think, you know, the -- perhaps the -- the best cases, in terms of support for our position, are the Pope and the Boyd cases. So Pope, you had a situation very similar to this where someone brought suit in a State court that -- where the injury had not occurred. The only connection was the railroad was doing business there. And in that case, this Court said this, Section 56, quote, \"establishes Petitioner's right to sue in Alabama. It provides that the employee may bring his suit wherever the carrier, quote, 'shall be doing business,'\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Murray",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We know that they get up to 10 percent of their revenue in the State of Montana, which, given that they operate in 28 States and a couple Canadian provinces, it has to be the case that that is disproportionately large compared to the other States in which they operate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But what is -- what is his state of residence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Murray",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0002-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Nelson, for example, living in North Dakota, would be headed to Washington, Texas, or Delaware to bring his claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-405",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "86c8545503432c2bb2f43fc8fe373ae3723e99d4b40cc1a68924adba0a1434ce",
      "evidence_id": "ev-86c854550343-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
